he network. [Data: Entities (3160, 3164); Relationships (6273)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"BPH is a key differential diagnosis because symptoms can overlap with prostate cancer\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) appears as a noncancerous condition that can produce urinary symptoms resembling those of prostate cancer. The relationship between BPH and prostate cancer stresses that overlapping symptoms may be difficult to distinguish based on symptoms alone, implying the need for further evaluation when symptoms appear. [Data: Entities (3394, 1076); Relationships (6728)]\\n\\nThis differential-diagnosis linkage reinforces the broader network pattern: symptoms are relevant signals, but they are neither necessary (given early symptom-free disease) nor sufficient (given overlap with BPH) to classify prostate cancer without additional testing. [Data: Entities (3160, 3394); Relationships (6273, 6728)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Race-linked disparities are central: Black males are described as higher risk with higher mortality, while some groups are diagnosed less often\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Race is represented as a clinical/demographic factor tied to differences in typical PSA levels and to variation in incidence and outcomes in the United States. The network explicitly uses White males as a comparator baseline for incidence and mortality discussions, especially when contrasting outcomes with Black males. [Data: Entities (3156, 3180); Relationships (6230)]\\n\\nBlack males are described as having a higher burden of prostate cancer than White males, including higher incidence, earlier onset patterns, more aggressive and/or more advanced presentation at diagnosis, and substantially higher mortality (about twice as likely to die compared with White males). The dataset also links Black males to discussion of PSA variation by race and suggests earlier and/or more frequent screening discussions with providers. [Data: Entities (3179, 3156); Relationships (6667, 6767)]\\n\\nIn contrast, Hispanic males and Asian males are described as being diagnosed with prostate cancer less often than both White and Black males. These relationships together position race-associated differences as a major organizing thread across detection, risk assessment, and outcomes. [Data: Entities (3181, 3182); Relationships (6231, 6232)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Diet and lifestyle are framed as risk modifiers: high-fat foods linked to increased risk; fruits/vegetables and plant-based foods linked to healthier patterns\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Diet and lifestyle are explicitly treated as a behavioral factor set associated with prostate cancer risk and aggressiveness, with the important caveat that the text does not claim any single diet definitively prevents or causes prostate cancer. Within that framework, eating high-fat foods is linked with increased risk, and meat and dairy products are repeatedly used as examples of high-fat foods associated with that increased risk. [Data: Entities (3157, 3187, 3188, 3189); Relationships (6271, 6279, 6280)]\\n\\nConversely, eating more fruits and vegetables is described as potentially reducing prostate cancer risk, and “plant-based foods” are recommended as a dietary pattern category that includes vegetables and whole grains. This positions plant-forward dietary patterns as part of survivorship/health guidance within the community’s information space. [Data: Entities (3203, 3204, 6309, 6328); Relationships (6292, 6293, 14475, 14477)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Weight and activity are connected to aggressiveness and mortality: obesity increases risk while weight loss/exercise and healthy weight are linked to better outcomes\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network connects body weight status to both the risk of developing prostate cancer and the risk of dying from it, stating that obesity increases these risks. [Data: Entities (3195); Relationships (6285)]\\n\\nIt also links weight loss and maintaining a healthy weight with potentially decreased aggressiveness and a lower likelihood of death from prostate cancer, and states that exercise may decrease aggressiveness. Together, these relationships present weight management and activity as modifiable factors associated with disease severity and outcomes. [Data: Entities (3194, 3398, 3190); Relationships (6283, 6770, 6769)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Advanced disease management emphasizes escalating therapy intensity and multiple treatment options, including clinical trials\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Advanced-stage prostate cancer is described as being treated with hormone therapy and other systemic therapies, and “advanced prostate cancer treatments” are presented as a set of options that includes hormone therapy, non-hormone therapies, clinical trials, and supportive care. This indicates a multi-modal approach to advanced disease and an explicit role for clinical trials in the care landscape. [Data: Entities (1076, 3520); Relationships (7155)]\\n\\nThe community also defines therapy in terms of intensity: as cancer invades deeper or spreads, therapy typically becomes more intensive, and “aggressive” therapy is described as stronger or more intense than other available options. This aligns with the explicit inclusion of metastatic classification elsewhere in the network and supports a stage/spread-driven escalation model for treatment selection. [Data: Entities (3413, 3415); Relationships (6790, 6809)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Expert roles (scientists, cancer researchers, health care professionals) shape knowledge, terminology, and clinical decision-making\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Scientists and cancer researchers are represented as the groups advancing knowledge over time and investigating causation and risk factors, with an explicit statement that the exact reason prostate cells become cancerous is not yet known. This frame